  "query_preview": "..\n\nHigh pressure will drifts off the Mid-Atlantic Coast today leading \nto increasing southwest return flow. Influence from this high \npressure will bring dry and around average temperatures this \nafternoon. Highs today should land in the low 80s for most the area. \nThere remains haze/smoke from wildfires hanging around to the west \nand parts of the state today that will pivot back through the area as\nwinds become southwesterly. Some improvements to the air quality are\nexpected compared to the last several days, but concentrations still\nmay be in the Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups AQI range per EGLE. \nThus, an Air Quality Alert remains in place through today. \n\nProspects for showers and thunderstorms increase tonight as a potent \nCanadian shortwave and cold front swing across the region. Models do \npoint to a vort max that will round the western CONUS ridge ahead of \nthis wave early this evening, but lack of instability and moisture \navailability should keep the area mostly dry until later tonight. \nHires models depict blossoming convection occuring west of Lake \nMichigan that arrives as a broken line into southeast Michigan. \nThere is some uncertainty in regards to timing with convection first \narriving between 04-06Z across the Saginaw Valley and spreading \nsoutheastward to the Detroit Metro region in the 10-12Z window. The \ngreater uncertainty remains the available instability in the 06-12Z \nwindow as convection is expected to arrive with the greater synoptic \nsupport outpacing the instability. This would lean towards a \nweakening of any more organized convection as it moves into southeast\nMichigan. Though, moisture quality is expected to improve with PWATs\nto near 2.00 inches along with increasing deep layer shear and \nsteepening mid- level lapse rates. This at least supports high PoPs \nfor showers with embedded thunderstorms that will pose an isolated \nsevere weather risk. Convective Outlook continues to carry a Marginal\nRisk for all of southeast Michigan with a small portion of Slight \nRisk across far northwest portions of the CWA. Damaging wind gusts \nand brief heavy downpours are the main threats. \n\nSystem's cold front will be trailing behind the lead wave of \nthunderstorms in the morning and push through during the day. \nDestabilization will be questionable post morning thunderstorms. If \nfrontal timing lags enough, additional thunderstorms will be \npossible that may pose an isolated severe risk. Favored area would \nbe far southeast portions of the CWA (roughly along and south of a \nPort Huron to Adrian line). \n\nPassage of the front will bring a noticeable drop in temperatures \nfor Wednesday with 850mb temperatures falling to below 10C resulting \nin daytime high temperatures in the low to mid 70s. Surface high \npressure should keep conditions mostly dry mid-week even with broad \ncyclonic flow aloft from the eastern Canadian closed mid-upper low. \nThe only exception may be across the Thumb where a few showers early \nWednesday may be possible as a shortwave drops across the north \ncentral Great Lakes. Michigan will remain under a dry and cooler \nnorthwest flow into the late week period while residing between the \neast Canadian trough and the western CONUS ridge. This brings \ntemperatures roughly around average to close out the work week while \nlimiting convective chances.",
